---
import FlightScene021 from "../../../components/studies/FlightScene021.svelte";
import FlightScene022 from "../../../components/studies/FlightScene022.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "flight-scene-instrument-2026-11-10",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section aria-labelledby="problem">
    <h2 id="problem">01 / Every study builds its own window into the game</h2>
    <p>
      A study about an engine plume, a cannon ray or a gravity fold has to show
      the thing in flight. Each one so far has built that view itself. The
      <a href="/lab/studies/ion-engines-2026-10-31">ion engine</a>
      streams, the
      <a href="/lab/studies/lhi-wasp#flight-demo">Wasp flight demo</a>, the
      <a href="/lab/studies/base-movement-ion">stabilizer flight</a>, the
      <a href="/lab/studies/gravity-coil-fx-2026-11-03">gravity fold flyby</a>
      and the
      <a href="/lab/studies/light-ion-cannon-2026-11-08">Light Ion Cannon</a>
      ray each chose their own background, effects and playback controls. Two
      shared a flight script and one borrowed the Motion instrument’s burns; the
      rest wrote their own. One drew the hull by hand, several flew a private
      approximation of the physics, and none composed every layer the game
      draws. The effect is judged against a different picture every time, and
      none of those pictures is quite the game.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Flight scene instrument is the Motion instrument’s sibling. It keeps
      the same burns and the same time slider, and the same full-window band
      that snaps into the reading flow. Where the Motion instrument draws
      graphs, this one gives the screen to a single large view of the game’s own
      layers: stars, ship, engine effects, the light surface and weapon fire,
      drawn by the production renderers from the production simulation. An agent
      configures the ship and the scene; the designer picks a burn, moves
      through time and looks. The Light Ion Cannon study planted the seed: it
      put a small game view in the Motion instrument’s path pane and repainted
      its light from the start of the burn whenever the cursor moved back. The
      scene has the game’s gravity and power limits, so a burn flies a different
      path here than on the Motion instrument’s graphs.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tension is between a picture that can be inspected and a picture that
      is true. The game’s light is not a function of the moment: the effects
      surface is fading pixel history, sparks and the camera carry state, and
      weapons fire by themselves only when a target is within their reach and
      the ship has power. A time slider asks for any instant on demand; the game
      can only arrive at an instant by flying there. How the instrument
      reconciles the two decides what a study can trust it to show.
    </p>
    <h3>Held fixed</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The Motion instrument’s burn programs B1, B2 and B3, their 14 seconds
        and the time cursor that selects an instant.
      </li>
      <li>
        The full-window, snapping study embed and the pane that holds the values
        under investigation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Production simulation and renderers, drawn in the game’s order. The
        instrument adds no ship art, effect or physics of its own, and later
        production changes show up in it. The order itself is written only
        inside the game’s flight screen today, so the instrument copies it until
        it is shared.
      </li>
      <li>
        The agent supplies the configuration; the designer adjusts only what the
        study exposes.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Left open</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The screen: how large the scene is, and where the burn row, time and
        playback sit around it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Time: what a jump to an instant shows of light that depends on the
        preceding moments, and whether the scene also plays.
      </li>
      <li>
        The camera: the game’s framing and zoom jumps, a fixed view, or a
        choice.
      </li>
      <li>
        The scene around the ship: what a configuration can change in the game’s
        arena (where the ship starts, which bodies are present, targets that
        make weapons fire) and which layers the designer can switch off to
        isolate one.
      </li>
      <li>Whether one ship is enough or a comparison belongs on the screen.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Outside this cycle</h3>
    <p>
      Moving earlier studies’ benches onto the instrument, a permanent Lab room
      for it, flying it by hand with the keyboard, and new burns that include
      weapon inputs are adjacent questions. The game has no fire button, so a
      burn stays an engine program.
    </p>
    <aside class="stamp" aria-labelledby="stamp">
      <h3 id="stamp">Question check · 10 November 2026</h3>
      <p>
        An independent reviewer with no part in the framing tested this section
        as first written. Grounded failed on how the section described what
        exists, not on whether it exists; the text above was corrected
        afterwards and has not been tested again.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Metric</th>
            <th scope="col">Result</th>
            <th scope="col">Reason</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">One subject</th>
            <td>Pass</td>
            <td>
              One object, the instrument. The five open items are facets of its
              screen, and four adjacent questions are named.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Grounded</th>
            <td>Fail</td>
            <td>
              Every fixed item exists and every open item can be shown. The
              section overstated how far the earlier views differed, left out
              the Light Ion Cannon study’s scene in the Motion instrument,
              called the draw order shared when it is written in one place, and
              listed the time cursor as fixed while leaving scrubbing open.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Consequence</th>
            <td>Pass</td>
            <td>
              Studies judge flight on one shared screen instead of building
              their own. The change is to the Lab, not the game, which the
              test’s usual wording does not cover.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="time">
    <h2 id="time">
      02.1 / Can the game’s light be reached by a jump? — yes, by flying there
    </h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Settled, 10 November 2026.</strong>
      A jump flies the burn again from its start and paints the whole flight’s
      light, so the frame it lands on is the frame playing would have shown. The
      scene both plays and jumps. The setting in this example’s menu stays as
      the evidence; the instrument itself has no such setting.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first example takes the time question, because every other choice
      rests on it. The hypothesis: a jump can show the frame the game would have
      shown, if the instrument flies the burn again from its start to reach it.
      Light on the effects surface halves every tenth of a second, so painting
      only the last second of that flight should be enough.
    </p>
    <p>
      Press Play, or drag Time to jump. The menu in the top-right corner sets
      what a jump paints: only the instant it lands on, the last second before
      it, or the whole flight. Compare the three at a moment when the engine or
      the cannon is lit, such as 1.67 s on B1.
    </p>
    <div class="bleed"><FlightScene021 client:visible /></div>
    <p>
      The scene is the game’s arena under Derelicts rules, with the encounter
      held off: sun, planets, boundary, starfield and the game camera, drawn in
      the flight screen’s order. The starter Wasp carries a Light Ion Cannon on
      its first hull mount and starts at rest where a run starts. Two wrecks are
      placed ahead of it so the cannon fires. Nothing is random. The screen
      borrows the Motion instrument’s size, burn row and slider; its layout is
      provisional.
    </p>
    <h3>Measured</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        At nine instants on B1, a jump painting the last second differs from one
        painting the whole flight in at most 7 of 2,073,600 pixels.
      </li>
      <li>
        Painting only the landing instant differs in 800 to 16,000 pixels
        whenever an engine or the cannon is lit: the plume and the ray’s
        afterglow are missing.
      </li>
      <li>
        A jump to the end of the burn takes about 0.2 s. Almost all of it is
        flying the simulation; painting the whole flight’s light adds about a
        seventh.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These figures come from one machine on 10 November 2026. Painting the
      whole flight runs the same code as playing, tick for tick, but the two
      were not compared pixel by pixel. The figures say a jump can be faithful;
      they do not say whether a still instant is a useful way to judge an effect
      that the player only ever sees moving.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two things show up that belong to later rounds. The game camera zooms out
      as the ship climbs away from the sun, so the ship is small for most of the
      burn. And the first wreck is destroyed within two seconds, after which the
      cannon has nothing to fire at.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="camera">
    <h2 id="camera">02.2 / What should the scene be looked at through?</h2>
    <p>
      The game camera frames the ship and the sun together and jumps between
      five zoom levels. That framing is part of what the player sees, but it
      leaves the ship about twenty pixels long for most of a burn, too small to
      judge a plume or a ray by. A closer look can be had in two ways that are
      not the same thing: enlarge the picture the game made, or move the camera
      in and let the game draw a new, sharper picture that no player ever gets.
    </p>
    <p>
      The switch in the scene’s top-left corner offers four answers. Compare
      them at 1.67 s on B1, with the engines and the cannon lit, and through the
      zoom jump near 2.5 s.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Game.</strong>
        The game camera, as in 02.1: true framing, true zoom jumps, small ship.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Game 3×.</strong>
        The same frame with its pixels enlarged three times around the ship.
        Everything is exactly what the player gets, only bigger; the zoom jumps
        remain.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Follow.</strong>
        The ship held at the centre at the game’s closest zoom level, 1.6. A
        scale the game does reach, without the framing or the jumps.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Follow 4×.</strong>
        The ship at the centre at a zoom the game never reaches. Ship art and
        light are drawn at that scale, so edges and afterglow are finer than in
        play.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="bleed"><FlightScene022 client:visible /></div>
    <p>
      The hypothesis is that one view cannot serve: framing and zoom jumps need
      Game, and an effect needs an enlargement, where Game 3× is the honest one
      and Follow 4× the flattering one. The light surface is tied to the camera,
      so switching the view flies the burn again. Scene, ship, wrecks and time
      model are those of 02.1. The switch sits on the scene only for this
      comparison; where it belongs is a layout question.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section><h2>03 / To be developed after 02</h2></section>
</StudyLayout>

<style is:global>
/* A page holding the instrument snaps to it while scrolling past. */
html:has(.bleed) {
  scroll-snap-type: y proximity;
  overflow-x: clip;
}
</style>
<style>
section > p,
section > ul {
  max-width: 72ch;
}
/* The prose column's width; the stamp's own type is smaller. */
.stamp {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  max-width: 41rem;
  margin-top: var(--spacing-lg);
  padding: var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-lg);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
  font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
}
.stamp table {
  border-collapse: collapse;
}
.stamp th,
.stamp td {
  padding: var(--spacing-sm) var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-sm) 0;
  text-align: left;
  vertical-align: top;
}
.stamp tbody th {
  white-space: nowrap;
  font-weight: normal;
  color: var(--color-text);
}
/* Full window band: the instrument fills it and snaps to its centre. */
.bleed {
  width: 100vw;
  height: 100svh;
  margin-inline: calc(50% - 50vw);
  scroll-snap-align: center;
}
</style>
